          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582783,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Elie Wiesel's Early Life</strong><br>\nElie Wiesel was born on September 30, 1928, in Sighet, a town in present-day Romania. He grew up in a Jewish family with three sisters and was deeply religious as a child. His early years were spent in a community where he learned about Jewish traditions and the importance of remembering history through storytelling, which influenced his later works.</p>\n\n<p><strong>World War II and the Holocaust</strong><br>\nDuring World War II, when Elie was 15, he and his family were deported to Auschwitz, a Nazi death camp. Wiesel's mother and younger sister perished there, while he and his father were later moved to Buchenwald, another concentration camp. These horrifying experiences defined his life and later his writings.</p>\n\n<p><strong>After the Liberation</strong><br>\nAfter being liberated from Buchenwald in 1945, Wiesel was sent to France with other orphaned Jewish children. There he learned French and eventually took up journalism. Struggling with the trauma of his experiences, he vowed not to speak about the Holocaust for ten years, a silence that ended with his writing.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "53bc5361-5ce5-43a9-a00f-57bbceee9cd0"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
