            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Wiesel as a Writer</strong><br>\nElie Wiesel is best known for his first book, \"Night,\" which detailed his experiences in the Holocaust. It is a stark, powerful account that has been translated into over 30 languages and is considered one of the seminal works on the subject, making the horror of the Holocaust personal and relatable for readers worldwide.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Academic and Teaching Career</strong><br>\nWiesel continued to write and also became a professor of humanities, teaching at Boston University. His academic work expanded his influence, allowing him to educate and inspire a new generation to remember and learn from the past, emphasizing the danger of indifference and the need for empathy and moral responsibility.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Political Activism and Advocacy</strong><br>\nAs a political activist, Wiesel advocated for many causes, including the remembrance of the Holocaust, the plight of Soviet Jews, human rights in South Africa and Nicaragua, and the victims of genocide in Darfur. He used his voice to fight against oppression and to champion human dignity.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
