          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Awards and Accolades</strong><br>\nWiesel's work earned him numerous awards, including the Nobel Peace Prize in 1986. The Nobel committee called him a \"messenger to mankind\" for his role in promoting peace, reconciliation, and human rights. He received over 100 honorary degrees and numerous other awards for his literary and human rights activities.</p>\n\n<p><strong>The Elie Wiesel Foundation for Humanity</strong><br>\nIn 1986, Wiesel and his wife, Marion, established the Elie Wiesel Foundation for Humanity to combat indifference, intolerance, and injustice through international dialogue and youth-focused programs that promote acceptance, understanding, and equality.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Legacy and Death</strong><br>\nElie Wiesel died on July 2, 2016, but his legacy endures through his writings, which continue to educate people about the Holocaust and its lessons. He left behind a message of the importance of memory and human compassion, urging future generations to work towards a world without hatred.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
